## Versioning and authority

The HyperMarkDown language follows Semantic Versioning (SemVer 2.0.0) and is
versioned independently of its software implementations. Editorial corrections
and clarifications that do not change the syntax or semantics of the language
are published as patch releases.

This specification is the authoritative definition of HyperMarkDown.
Implementations and accompanying documentation are derived from the standard,
rather than defining it.

## Terms

| Term | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| **card** | A card is the fundamental unit of knowledge in HyperMarkDown, represented as an individually addressable document. Cards within a namespace can be interconnected to form a structured knowledge base. |
| **HyperMarkDown card** | A card with the extension `.hmd`. It is the only kind of card in which HyperMarkDown syntax is recognized. |
| **Markdown card** | A card with the extension `.md` or `.markdown`. It can be linked to and embedded, but HyperMarkDown syntax is never recognized in it. |
| **vault** | A directory that contains a `.hmd/` directory, which holds the vault's configuration. |
| **namespace root** | The directory where the vault's cards start, named by the `location_path` setting. |
| **namespace** | A logical collection of cards and modules that together form a knowledge base with a shared naming and addressing space. |
| **module** | A directory within a namespace that groups cards and provides a naming scope. |
| **folder note** | A card named `index`, which stands for its directory. |
| **wikilink** | A link in double square brackets, such as `[[setup]]`, optionally with display text after a pipe. |
| **embed** | A wikilink with `!` in front, which is expanded in place. |
| **block anchor** | A trailing `^id` that names a block. |
| **fragment** | What follows `#` in a target: a heading's text, or `^` and a block anchor's identifier. |
| **unqualified reference** | A target with no leading `/`, `./`, or `../`, which is searched for. |
| **cell** | A fenced block that a tool runs or draws. |

## The example vault

Throughout this specification, the following example vault illustrates the
organization of cards and modules, name resolution, embeds, and diagnostics.

```text
handbook/                      the vault
  .hmd/config.toml             location_path = "wiki"
  wiki/                        the namespace root
    index.hmd                  folder note of the root, published
    setup.hmd                  # Setup, ## Linux ^setup-linux, ## macOS
    guides/                    a module
      index.hmd                folder note of guides/
      install.hmd
      tokens.hmd
    reference/                 a module
      tokens.hmd
      api.md                   a Markdown card
```

`guides/install.hmd`:

```markdown
---
import:
  - from /reference import api
---

# Install

Follow [[setup#linux]], or embed it:

![[setup#^setup-linux]]

Every request carries one of the [[tokens]]; the endpoints are in [[api]].
Deploying is described in [[deploy]].
```

How each of its references resolves:

| Reference | Result | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `[[setup#linux]]` | `setup.hmd`, the heading `Linux` | Found at the namespace root, the last parent directory searched; the fragment matches the heading's normalized identifier |
| `![[setup#^setup-linux]]` | the `Linux` section of `setup.hmd` | An anchor on a heading line embeds that heading's section |
| `[[tokens]]` | `guides/tokens.hmd` | Found in the card's own directory, which is searched before its parents |
| `[[api]]` | `reference/api.md` | A named import binds the name |
| `[[deploy]]` | unresolved: a warning | The referenced card does not yet exist. Unresolved wikilinks allow authors to reference concepts before creating their corresponding cards, following the traditional wiki approach. |

`setup.hmd` also links `[[tokens]]`. That card sits at the namespace root, which
has no parent to search and holds no `tokens`, so the recursive search runs and
finds two cards. `hmd lint` reports:

```text
guides/install.hmd:13:27: warning[HMD001] [[deploy]] does not resolve to a page
setup.hmd:9:51: error[HMD002] [[tokens]] matches 2 pages; qualify it (candidates: guides/tokens.hmd, reference/tokens.hmd)

1 error(s), 1 warning(s)
```

## Conformance to the standard

Independent implementations of HyperMarkDown must interpret documents according
to the syntax and semantics defined by this standard. Given the same document
and vault context, implementations must recognize the same language constructs,
resolve references to the same targets, and assign the same meaning to those
constructs.

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD",
"SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this
document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14
([RFC 2119](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119),
[RFC 8174](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8174)) when, and only when, they
appear in all capitals, as shown here.

- A **conforming implementation** MUST accept every document this specification
  defines as well-formed, MUST assign it the meaning given here, and MUST report
  each condition listed under [Diagnostics](#diagnostics) with the identifier
  and severity given there.
- An implementation MAY provide constructs beyond this specification, but MUST
  NOT assign a different meaning to any construct defined here.
- **CommonMark compatibility.** HyperMarkDown inherits the syntax and
  semantics of [CommonMark 0.31.2](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.31.2/). Where
  HyperMarkDown explicitly extends or modifies these rules, this standard takes
  precedence. All other CommonMark rules remain unchanged.
- **What must agree with CommonMark in 0.1.** An implementation MUST find the
  constructs where a CommonMark reading of the document puts them, within the
  known divergence described under [Lexical model](#lexical-model). How it renders the inherited Markdown is
  not part of conformance in this version.
- **Determinism.** Given the same documents and configuration, an
  implementation MUST produce identical reference resolutions, embed
  expansions, and diagnostics, regardless of the operating system or
  filesystem traversal order.
- **Verification.** The language standard serves as the basis for automated
  tests. The test dataset, at `examples/conformance/cases/`, provides
  executable examples of the behavior defined by this standard: valid and
  invalid documents and their expected interpretation. It does not constitute
  an exhaustive definition of the language. The absence of a test case does
  not make a rule optional; the standard remains authoritative independently
  of the coverage of the test dataset. Any discrepancy between the standard,
  the test dataset, and an implementation is investigated and resolved in
  favor of the standard.

Renaming a `.md` file to `.hmd` keeps it syntactically valid, but can change
how it is read: `[[foo]]` is literal text in CommonMark and a wikilink in
HyperMarkDown, and a leading `---` block that matches the grammar below is
frontmatter.

HyperMarkDown defines the syntax and semantics of documents independently of
their presentation. Implementations may render documents differently, provided
they preserve their defined meaning.

## Documents

A HyperMarkDown document consists of an optional YAML frontmatter block
followed by Markdown content, including the extensions defined by this
standard. Documents are stored as UTF-8 encoded text files.

HyperMarkDown recognizes three card extensions: `.hmd`, `.md`, and
`.markdown`. Extensions are compared exactly, case included, as names are:
`shout.HMD` and `notes.MD` have no card extension and are ignored. When multiple cards in the same directory have the same name but
different extensions, references without an explicit extension resolve
according to this priority order:

```text
.hmd  .md  .markdown
```

- A `.hmd` file is a **HyperMarkDown card**: an OPTIONAL frontmatter block
  followed by CommonMark content. A card with no frontmatter and no
  HyperMarkDown construct is well-formed. Where this specification says "card"
  without saying what it contains, it means a HyperMarkDown card.
- A `.md` or `.markdown` file is a **Markdown card**, specified under
  [Markdown cards](#markdown-cards).
- Where two files compete for one name, the earlier extension wins; see
  [Resolving a reference within a directory](#resolving-a-reference-within-a-directory).
- Files with any other extension MUST be ignored: they are neither link
  targets nor inputs to checking.

A file is a **card** if, and only if, it satisfies all of the following
conditions:

- It is located within the namespace root (see [The namespace](#the-namespace)).
- It has a recognized card extension.
- It does not belong to a nested vault (see [The namespace](#the-namespace)).
- It contains valid UTF-8 text ([Encoding](#encoding)).

Wherever this specification speaks of a card, a candidate, or a valid card, it
means a file that satisfies these conditions.

### Encoding

- A card MUST be encoded in UTF-8. A file with a card extension that is not
  valid UTF-8 MUST be reported as an error at line 1, column 1
  ([HMD019](#diagnostics)). It is then not a card: no HyperMarkDown syntax is
  recognized in it, and it is not a link target.
- A single byte-order mark (`EF BB BF`) at the start of a card MUST be
  ignored.[^byte-order-mark] Byte 0 of the card, wherever this specification
  names it, is the first byte after it.
- A line ends at `\n`, at `\r\n`, or at a `\r` not followed by `\n`, as in
  CommonMark.

[^byte-order-mark]: A byte-order mark is invisible in most editors. If it were
    not ignored, it would hide a frontmatter block, which must begin at byte 0.

### Markdown cards

Markdown cards allow existing Markdown documents to participate in a
HyperMarkDown knowledge base without modifying their content or interpreting it
as HyperMarkDown.

They can be referenced and embedded by other cards, while their body retains
its CommonMark semantics.

- HyperMarkDown syntax MUST NOT be recognized in a Markdown card. Its body
  yields no wikilink, no embed and no block anchor; a `[[...]]` in it is text.
- It MAY open with a frontmatter block under the same fence rule. An
  implementation MUST expose the mapping and MUST NOT interpret any of its keys,
  the four reserved keys included. A block that does not parse as a YAML
  mapping is body text, not an error.
- Its headings are matched by the rule under [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors).
  It has no block anchors.
- No diagnostic is raised from its body.
- An embed of a Markdown card transcludes its body as CommonMark, with no
  HyperMarkDown expansion inside it.

## Lexical model

This section defines where HyperMarkDown syntax is recognized within a
document, which regions are excluded from recognition, and how the source
positions of recognized elements are represented.

- HyperMarkDown syntax MUST NOT be recognized within fenced code blocks (both
  ` ``` ` and `~~~`), inline code spans, or HTML comments (`<!-- ... -->`).
  These are the only excluded regions: their contents never yield a link, an
  embed, or an anchor.[^excluded-regions]
- Indented code blocks are not excluded: HyperMarkDown syntax inside them MUST
  be recognized.[^indented-code]
- Raw inline HTML is not excluded: a hand-written `<code>` element is treated
  like any other text.
- Every construct MUST record a span of `(start_offset, end_offset, line,
  column)`. Offsets count UTF-8 bytes from the start of the card, starting at
  0. `line` and `column` start at 1, and `column` counts Unicode code
  points.[^code-point-columns]
- Recognition MUST NOT depend on filesystem traversal order, on the contents of
  any other file, or on any state outside the card's own bytes. Everything
  cross-file belongs to name resolution.

**Known divergence.** A line-oriented masking scanner differs from a full
CommonMark parser on pathological input, such as a wikilink inside an HTML
block or a fence opened inside a blockquote. This is permitted in 0.1. It is not
a license to diverge on ordinary input.[^divergence]

[^excluded-regions]: An implementation may achieve this by identifying and
    masking excluded regions before scanning the document for HyperMarkDown
    syntax. Masking that preserves byte offsets keeps every source span exact.

[^indented-code]: A four-space indent is also how a callout body and a footnote
    continuation are written, so excluding indented code would silently drop
    real links from ordinary prose. An author who needs code has fences.

[^code-point-columns]: Columns count code points so that a position is the
    same in every implementation, whatever its string type.

[^divergence]: The divergence is tolerated because the set of HyperMarkDown
    constructs is small, and every divergence can be diagnosed.

## Grammar

The grammar below defines wikilinks, embeds, and their reference targets. It
applies wherever HyperMarkDown syntax is recognized, as defined in
[Lexical model](#lexical-model). It is written in ABNF
([RFC 5234](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5234)), with the case-sensitive
strings of [RFC 7405](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7405). `ALPHA` and `DIGIT` are
RFC 5234's core rules: ASCII letters and digits only. A character is a Unicode
scalar value.

```abnf
embed            = "!" wikilink
wikilink         = "[[" target [ "|" display ] "]]"
target           = page-ref [ fragment ]

page-ref         = absolute / relative / unqualified
absolute         = "/" [ segment *( "/" segment ) [ "/" ] ]
relative         = ( "./" / "../" )
                   [ rel-component *( "/" rel-component ) [ "/" ] ]
rel-component    = segment / "." / ".."
unqualified      = ( bare-name / unqualified-path ) [ "/" ]
bare-name        = segment
unqualified-path = segment 1*( "/" segment )
segment          = 1*seg-char
seg-char         = %x00-09 / %x0B-0C / %x0E-22 / %x24-2E / %x30-39
                 / %x3B-5A / %x5F-7B / %x7D-D7FF / %xE000-10FFFF
                 ; any character except "/", "\", ":", "[", "]", "|",
                 ; "#", "^", and a line ending

fragment         = "#" heading-text / "#^" block-id
heading-text     = heading-first *heading-char
heading-first    = %x00-09 / %x0B-0C / %x0E-5A / %x5F-7B / %x7D-D7FF
                 / %xE000-10FFFF
                 ; any character except "[", "\", "]", "|", "^",
                 ; and a line ending
heading-char     = %x00-09 / %x0B-0C / %x0E-5A / %x5E-7B / %x7D-D7FF
                 / %xE000-10FFFF
                 ; any character except "[", "\", "]", "|", and a line ending
block-id         = ( ALPHA / DIGIT ) 0*63( ALPHA / DIGIT / "_" / "-" )

display          = 1*display-char
display-char     = %x00-09 / %x0B-0C / %x0E-5A / %x5E-D7FF
                 / %xE000-10FFFF
                 ; any character except "[", "\", "]", and a line ending
```

- A **bare name** is exactly one `segment`. A page reference with two or more
  segments and no leading `/`, `./`, or `../` is an **unqualified path**. Both
  are **unqualified references** and use the same search; only a bare name can
  match a named import.
- The **reserved characters** in a `target` are `[`, `]`, `|`, `#`, `^`, `:`,
  `\`, and a line ending, and `/` everywhere except as the separator between
  segments.
- **A backslash MUST NOT appear anywhere in a wikilink**: not in its path,
  its fragment, or its display. A wikilink that contains one is malformed, an
  error ([HMD010](#diagnostics)). A backslash is never a path separator, on
  any platform; paths are written with `/` only.
- A `segment` MUST NOT be `.` or `..`. Only an explicit relative reference may
  contain them, and they follow Unix path semantics: the reference is
  normalized before it is resolved, and the namespace containment rule applies
  to the result.
- A page reference that ends in `/` names a directory, and resolves only to
  that directory's folder note. So do `./` and `../` with no following
  component, which name the current and the parent directory, and a relative
  reference whose last component is `.` or `..`. `[[/]]` names the namespace
  root, and resolves to the root's folder note. A reference that names a
  directory is not a bare name, so it never matches a named import.
- In a `heading-text`, `#` MAY appear anywhere and `^` anywhere but first; both
  are ordinary characters of the heading text. A `^` directly after the
  fragment's `#` makes the fragment a `block-id` instead. Neither character
  survives normalization (see [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors)), so
  `[[setup#C# tips]]` and `[[setup#C tips]]` both reach `## C# tips`.
- A `heading-text` and a `display` MUST each contain at least one character
  that is not whitespace. Otherwise the wikilink is malformed, an error
  ([HMD010](#diagnostics)).
- Whitespace at the start and the end of a `page-ref` is not part of it:
  `[[ setup ]]` is `[[setup]]`.
- A `page-ref` MAY end in a card extension. An implementation MUST strip it
  before matching and MUST then bind only that extension: `[[owl.md]]` reaches
  `owl.md` even where `owl.hmd` shadows it.
- **No escape mechanism exists in this version.** A target that needs a
  reserved character is malformed, an error ([HMD010](#diagnostics)).[^no-escape]
- The `namespace:path` form is reserved for references across namespaces, not
  part of the 0.1 grammar; see [Not in this version](#not-in-this-version).
  Because `:` is a reserved character, every such target is malformed.

`[`, `]`, and `|` delimit the wikilink, and `#` and `^` introduce its fragment,
so none of them can be part of a name.

[^no-escape]: The missing escape is a known gap, not a design position.

### Link candidates

- A **link candidate** begins at each `[[` outside an excluded region. It ends
  at the first `]]` after it, provided no `[`, `]`, or line ending comes before
  that `]]`. A `!` directly before the `[[` makes the candidate an embed.
- A candidate MUST match `wikilink`, or `embed` when it has the `!`. One that
  does not is malformed, an error ([HMD010](#diagnostics)), reported at its
  first character.
- A `[[` that begins no candidate is ordinary text. An implementation MAY warn
  that it looks like an unfinished wikilink; such a warning is not one of the
  diagnostics of this specification.
- A backslash escapes nothing here: `\[[x]]` is a wikilink, because the
  grammar has no escape mechanism. The backslash before `[[` is text, not part
  of the wikilink, so the ban on a backslash inside a wikilink does not apply
  to it. To show a construct without invoking it,
  put it in a code span or a fenced block.
- Candidates are found in the text whatever CommonMark inline syntax surrounds
  them. A candidate inside a CommonMark link's text or destination, or inside
  emphasis, is still a candidate. Only an excluded region stops recognition.

In `[[[a]]]`, the first `[[` is followed by `[`, so it begins no candidate, and
the second begins the candidate `[[a]]`; the last `]` is text. In the math
`$[[1,2],[3,4]]$`, a `]` comes before the first `]]`, so no candidate begins
and the text stays as written.

### Block anchors

A **block anchor** is a trailing `^block-id` at the end of a block's last line,
followed only by spaces or tabs and the line ending:

```abnf
block-anchor     = 1*( %x20 / %x09 ) "^" block-id *( %x20 / %x09 )
                 ; one or more spaces or tabs, the anchor, then
                 ; optional spaces or tabs before the line ending
```

- Spaces and tabs may precede a block anchor, and at least one of them is
  REQUIRED. The source position of a block anchor begins at the `^`
  character, excluding any preceding whitespace.
- The anchor and the whitespace around it are not part of the block's
  content, and MUST NOT be rendered.
- An identifier's scope is its card. Two cards can use the same identifier;
  two anchors in one card MUST NOT, and a duplicate is an error
  ([HMD006](#diagnostics)), reported at the later anchor's `^`.
- An anchor at the end of a heading's line names that heading. Its block is the
  heading's whole section; see [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion).

### Frontmatter block

A card MAY open with a frontmatter block. A frontmatter block begins and ends
with a delimiter line containing `---`. The opening delimiter MUST begin at
byte 0 of the card.

- An opening delimiter without a corresponding closing delimiter does not
  establish a frontmatter block. The document is interpreted as ordinary
  Markdown.
- The YAML document terminator `...` is not a HyperMarkDown frontmatter
  closing delimiter.
- An empty frontmatter block represents an empty mapping.
- Otherwise the block MUST parse as YAML and MUST be a mapping at the top
  level. If it does not, it is an error ([HMD009](#diagnostics)).
- Exactly four top-level keys are **reserved**: `tags`, `use`, `import`, and
  `nav`, specified under [Frontmatter](#frontmatter).[^mechanisms]
- Every other key is the author's and MUST NOT be interpreted. An implementation
  MUST still expose the whole mapping to its consumers.

[^mechanisms]: The language ships mechanisms, not vocabulary: the four keys it
    needs, and nothing that would collide with an author's own.

## Heading anchors

A heading fragment names a heading by its text. Both sides are normalized
before they are compared, so a link need not repeat a heading's capitalization
or punctuation. Link by heading text where the link should read naturally;
anchor a heading whose links must survive a rename, or whose text is awkward
to type.

- A **heading** is an ATX heading (`# Setup`) or a setext heading (`Setup`
  over a line of `===` or `---`), as CommonMark defines them. Both are
  addressable. A line inside an excluded region (see
  [Lexical model](#lexical-model)) is never a heading.
- A heading's **text** is its content as a reader sees it, with inline markup
  removed: `# The *fast* path` has the text `The fast path`. A closing run of
  `#` and a trailing block anchor are not part of it.
- The **normalized heading identifier** of a text, a heading's or a
  fragment's, is computed in four steps:[^own-normalization]

```text
heading_identifier(text):
  1. normalize to Unicode NFC
  2. apply Unicode full case folding
  3. replace every character that is not a letter, a mark, or a number
     (general categories L, M, N) with a space
  4. collapse every run of spaces into one, and trim both ends
```

- A `heading-text` fragment MUST match the first heading, in document order,
  whose normalized identifier equals the fragment's. A fragment that matches no
  heading is an error ([HMD004](#diagnostics)).
- Letters and digits of every script are kept. `Ошибка 404`, `Café`, and `数据`
  each have an identifier of their own, and accents are significant.
- A heading whose normalized identifier equals that of an earlier heading in
  the same card MUST be reported as a warning, unless it carries a block anchor
  ([HMD011](#diagnostics)). A fragment reaches the first of them. An anchor
  reaches a later one: a second `## Linux ^linux-arm` is linked as
  `#^linux-arm`. Adding the anchor is the remedy, so it ends the warning.
- A heading whose normalized identifier is empty, such as one made only of
  symbols, cannot be addressed through a heading reference. It can be reached
  by an anchor. Empty heading identifiers are excluded from duplicate heading
  detection and do not produce HMD011.
- How a renderer turns a heading into an HTML `id`, or a fragment into a URL, is
  not part of the language. Whatever it chooses, it MUST rewrite each heading
  link to the `id` it gave the heading that link matched.

In the example vault, `[[setup#linux]]`, `[[setup#Linux]]`, and
`[[setup#LINUX!]]` all reach `## Linux`.

[^own-normalization]: The matching agrees with Obsidian's, which also ignores
    case and punctuation. The specification states the normalization itself
    rather than naming a library, so no implementation has to copy another's
    behavior.

## The namespace

The namespace is a single rooted subtree. In version 0.1 nothing outside it is
addressable by a page reference. Ordinary Markdown links still address files
and URLs outside it.

- The **vault** is the nearest ancestor directory that contains a `.hmd/`
  directory. Nothing else marks one; a `.git` directory does
  not.[^one-marker] A tree with no `.hmd/` above it has no vault, and its
  namespace root has to be named directly.
- By default, the **namespace root** is the vault directory itself. The
  optional `location_path` setting in `.hmd/config.toml` specifies it as a
  path relative to the vault directory; see
  [Project configuration](#project-configuration).
- An implementation MUST offer a way to name the namespace root directly,
  overriding both the search for a vault and the `location_path` setting.
- A card's path is written relative to the namespace root. What makes a file
  a card is defined under [Documents](#documents).
- A resolved target MUST lie inside the namespace root. One that does not is
  an error ([HMD003](#diagnostics)).
- Path normalization MUST happen before that containment check, and symbolic
  links MUST NOT be followed out of the root.
- Vaults may be nested. A directory below the namespace root that contains its
  own `.hmd/` is a nested vault, and it establishes an independent namespace.
  Cards in a nested vault do not belong to the enclosing namespace, whether as
  a page, a resolution candidate, or an import target, and reference
  resolution does not cross vault boundaries in either direction.
- Each vault defines its namespace root through `location_path`. The
  `location_path` of an enclosing vault MUST NOT resolve into a nested vault,
  and its namespace excludes all nested vaults and their contents. Nested
  vaults are thus allowed, and each card still belongs to exactly one
  namespace, even where the directory trees are physically nested.

A directory inside the namespace is a **module**: the unit that scopes a name
and inherits configuration. A directory MAY carry a **folder note**, a card
named `index` with any card extension. A reference to the directory and a
reference to its explicit `index` path resolve to that one card.
Configuration inherits from `index.hmd` only; a Markdown folder note's
frontmatter is not interpreted, so it sets nothing for its subtree.

In the example vault, `handbook/` is the vault, `wiki/` is the namespace root,
and `[[guides]]` reaches `guides/index.hmd`.

[^one-marker]: One marker has one meaning: a tree inside a git repository is
    not a vault until it says so.

## Name resolution

Name resolution is the process of locating a card referenced by a wikilink or
an embed.

HyperMarkDown uses Unix-style paths with forward slashes (`/`) as directory
separators, regardless of the underlying operating system; a backslash is
never a separator (see [Grammar](#grammar)). Reference names and
filesystem names MUST be normalized to Unicode NFC before
comparison.[^nfc-names] They MUST then be compared code point
by code point: case is significant on every platform, whatever the filesystem
does.

[^nfc-names]: The same name can be stored in two Unicode forms, and a reference
    must resolve the same way on every platform.

Relative paths are resolved from the directory containing the referring
document, not from the working directory of the application.

Absolute paths begin at the namespace root. Explicit relative paths begin with
`./` or `../`. References without these prefixes are unqualified and are
resolved according to the search rules defined below.

### Resolving a reference within a directory

Every phase of resolution looks a reference up in a directory the same way, so
a folder note behaves the same everywhere.

```text
bind(dir, parts, ext = none, directory_only = false):
    base := normalize(dir / join(parts))

    if base violates namespace boundaries:
        return Escapes

    exts := [ext] if ext is specified
            else [".hmd", ".md", ".markdown"]

    if not directory_only:
        for e in exts:
            file := base + e
            if file is a valid card:
                return Resolved(file)

    for e in exts:
        note := base / ("index" + e)
        if note is a valid card:
            return Resolved(note)

    return NotFound
```

A reference without an explicit extension follows the standard extension
priority. A reference with an explicit extension considers only cards with that
extension. References identifying a directory directly, such as `[[./]]` and
`[[../]]`, resolve exclusively to its folder note.

- Every candidate path MUST be checked against the namespace boundaries —
  normalization, symbolic links, and nested vaults included — before it is
  accepted. A boundary violation is `Escapes`, an error
  ([HMD003](#diagnostics)), and MUST NOT be treated as an ordinary missing card.
- A **valid card** is a card as defined under [Documents](#documents).
- A target naming a directory MUST resolve to that directory's folder note, so
  the directory form and the explicit `index` form reach the same card.
- Two files in one directory whose names differ only in their card extension
  claim one name. The one with the earliest extension is the card at that name;
  every other is **shadowed**: still a card, never bound by a reference that
  writes no extension, bound by one that writes its extension. Each shadowed
  file MUST be reported once, as a warning at its line 1, column 1
  ([HMD018](#diagnostics)). Folder notes shadow one another the same way.
- If a card and a module's folder note claim the same name as siblings, the
  card is the one at that name, in every phase, and the folder note is
  **shadowed**: still reachable by its `index` path and by a direct directory
  reference. The pair MUST be reported once, as a warning
  ([HMD012](#diagnostics)).

### Resolution algorithm

An absolute or explicit relative path is followed directly. An unqualified
reference is searched for, and resolves to exactly one card or to a failure
that can be diagnosed. The search checks named imports first. It then looks in
the referring card's own directory and in each parent directory up to the
namespace root, then in the directories imported as search paths. Only if all
of those find nothing does it run one recursive search of the namespace.

```text
resolve(target, source, root):
    ext     := explicit card extension, if present
    stem    := target.page-ref without ext
    dir_ref := stem ends with "/"
    origin  := dirname(source)
    imports := named_imports(source)
    paths   := wildcard_imports(source)
    enabled := autodiscovery(source)
    mode    := discovery_mode(source)

    # Absolute reference

    if stem starts with "/":
        parts := non-empty path components after the leading "/"
        return bind(root, parts, ext, directory_only = dir_ref)
               or Unresolved

    # Explicit relative reference

    if stem starts with "./" or "../":
        base := normalize(origin / stem)

        if base violates namespace boundaries:
            return Escapes

        if dir_ref or the last component of stem is "." or "..":
            return bind(base, [], ext, directory_only = true)
                   or Unresolved

        return bind(dirname(base), [basename(base)], ext)
               or Unresolved

    # Unqualified reference

    parts := non-empty components of split(stem, "/")

    # Phase 0: Named imports

    if not dir_ref and size(parts) == 1 and parts[0] in imports:
        card := imports[parts[0]]

        if ext is absent or extension(card) == ext:
            return Resolved(card)

    # Phase 1: Current directory and parents

    for dir in ancestors_inclusive(origin, root):
        result := bind(dir, parts, ext, directory_only = dir_ref)

        if result != NotFound:
            return result

    # Phase 2: Imported search paths

    matches := []

    for dir in paths:
        result := bind(dir, parts, ext, directory_only = dir_ref)

        if result is Escapes:
            if size(matches) == 0:
                return Escapes
            report HMD003 for this import path
            continue

        if result is Resolved:
            append distinct result to matches

    if size(matches) > 0:
        report HMD016 for subsequent shadowed matches
        return first(matches)

    # Phase 3: Recursive search

    if enabled == false:
        return Unresolved

    scope := origin if mode == "recursive" else root

    candidates := valid_cards_below(scope)

    if dir_ref:
        candidates := folder notes among candidates

    if ext is specified:
        candidates := candidates with extension ext
    else:
        candidates := candidates excluding
                      extension-shadowed cards

    matches := distinct candidates whose addressable
               path components end with parts

    if size(matches) == 1:
        return Resolved(first(matches))

    if size(matches) > 1:
        return Ambiguous(sorted(matches))

    return Unresolved
```

- `ancestors_inclusive(origin, root)` is `origin`, then each parent directory in
  turn, up to and including `root`.
- `autodiscovery(source)` is the source card's effective `use` toggle for
  `autodiscovery`; `discovery_mode(source)` is the project's `mode`, under
  [Project configuration](#project-configuration), which no card can change.
- A card's **addressable path components** are its root-relative components
  without its card extension; a folder note also has the components of its
  directory, so that an unqualified reference means the same thing in the
  recursive search as in the card's own directory and its parents. A folder
  note shadowed by a sibling card ([HMD012](#diagnostics)) does not have its
  directory's components: it is not a candidate by its directory's name, and
  stays reachable by its `index` path.

In the example vault, `[[tokens]]` in `guides/install.hmd` stops at Phase 1 in
`guides/`. The same reference in `setup.hmd` reaches Phase 3 and is ambiguous.

### Rules the algorithm must satisfy

Searching the card's own directory and its parents answers *what does this
reference mean here*, which must be predictable. The recursive search answers
*does this reference exist anywhere*: a convenience, which is allowed to fail
loudly. It runs only after every nearer phase has found nothing, so every match
it produces lies outside them, and the phases never disagree.

- **Order.** Resolution MUST follow the phase order above. A named import can
  shadow a local card. A wildcard import MUST NOT override a named import or a
  match in the card's own directory or its parents, but adding one can change a
  reference that previously resolved through the recursive search.
- **Containment.** Every phase operates within the source card's namespace.
  Paths MUST be normalized, and symbolic links validated, before a target is
  accepted; a target outside the namespace is `Escapes`
  ([HMD003](#diagnostics)), in whichever phase it is met.[^escape-loud]
- **Explicit extensions.** A reference that writes a card extension considers
  only cards with that extension, in every phase. An extension-shadowed card
  stays addressable this way, the recursive search included. A named import
  whose card has a different extension does not apply, and the search goes on
  to the next phase.
- **The card's own directory and its parents.** The search MUST run from that
  directory outward to the namespace root, inclusive of both ends, and each
  directory MUST be probed **non-recursively**. It never searches sideways into
  a sibling module.
- **Imported search paths.** They MUST be probed in declaration order,
  non-recursively. The first successful resolution through imported search
  paths determines the resolved target, but every later path MUST still be
  probed: each distinct card it finds is shadowed, and MUST be reported as a
  warning ([HMD016](#diagnostics)).[^import-warning] Two search paths that
  reach the same card are not a collision. An invalid subsequent import path
  does not invalidate an already resolved reference; invalid import paths
  remain subject to their own diagnostic rules, so a later path that crosses
  the namespace boundary is reported ([HMD003](#diagnostics)) without changing
  the result.
- **The recursive search.** It MUST run only when the earlier phases found
  nothing, and MUST run at most once. When autodiscovery is on, it can resolve
  a unique match in a sibling module or anywhere else in its scope.
- **Folder notes.** A reference that names a directory directly resolves only
  to its folder note. A folder note stays addressable by both its directory's
  name and its explicit `index` path, in the recursive search exactly as in
  binding. The priority is the same too: where a card and a module's folder
  note claim one name, the card is the candidate for that name, and the folder
  note is reached by its `index` path.
- **Candidate identity.** Candidates are cards, not names: several matching
  paths or addressable names that refer to one card are one candidate, and MUST
  NOT make a reference ambiguous.
- **Ranking.** In the recursive search all matches rank equally. A shallower
  match MUST NOT beat a deeper one, and more than one match MUST be reported as
  an error rather than resolved ([HMD002](#diagnostics)).[^qualify]
- **Candidate ordering.** Candidate paths are ordered lexicographically by
  Unicode code point, following the applicable name normalization rules.
  Ordering is independent of locale, operating system, and filesystem
  traversal order. This applies wherever candidate ordering affects
  observable results or diagnostics, such as a candidate list.
- **Forward references.** An unresolved target is **not** a failure. It is a
  forward reference, reported as a warning ([HMD001](#diagnostics)).[^forward]
- **Fragments.** The card is resolved first, and its heading or block fragment
  after; see [Fragments](#fragments).

[^escape-loud]: Treating a boundary violation as a missing card would hide a
    link that points outside the namespace.

[^import-warning]: An earlier import silently winning is easy to write by
    accident, hence the warning.

[^qualify]: The remedy for an ambiguity is to qualify the link, not to
    memorize a tie-break. Ordered candidates make a diagnostic byte-identical
    across platforms.

[^forward]: Writing forward is how a knowledge base grows.

### Path-suffix resolution

An unqualified reference can consist of a single name or a sequence of path
components.

During the recursive search, the reference is matched against the ending path
components of candidate cards. This allows a card to be referenced without
specifying its complete path within the namespace.

For example, consider a namespace containing the card
`science/physics/thermodynamics.hmd`.

| Reference | Interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| `[[/science/physics/thermodynamics]]` | Absolute path from the namespace root |
| `[[./physics/thermodynamics]]` | Explicit relative path, from a card in `science/` |
| `[[physics/thermodynamics]]` | Unqualified path |
| `[[thermodynamics]]` | Unqualified name |

During the recursive search, both unqualified references can resolve to the same
card. From a card outside `science/`, where neither the card's own directory nor
its parents hold them, they both reach `science/physics/thermodynamics.hmd`
when the project's `mode` is `both`, the default, provided no other card in the
namespace ends in the same path components.

Matching operates on complete path components rather than arbitrary string
suffixes: `[[dynamics]]` does not reach `thermodynamics.hmd`, and
`[[sics/thermodynamics]]` does not reach `physics/thermodynamics.hmd`. The
existing search scope, resolution precedence, and ambiguity rules apply.

### Fragments

Once a card is resolved, its fragment is matched against that card.

- A `heading-text` fragment MUST match by the rule under
  [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors); no match is an error ([HMD004](#diagnostics)).
- A `block-id` fragment MUST match, character for character and case
  included, the identifier of a block anchor in the resolved card; no match is
  an error ([HMD005](#diagnostics)).
- A fragment MUST NOT influence which card is resolved.

## Frontmatter

Configuration has two levels. A card says **whether** a feature applies to it;
project configuration says **how** that feature behaves. The order of the
resolution phases is the same everywhere. A card may explicitly enable or
disable the recursive search through its frontmatter configuration. In the
absence of an explicit setting, the inherited configuration applies, with the
precedence given under [Project configuration](#project-configuration).

```yaml
---
tags: [area/auth, status/accepted]
use: [no_autodiscovery]
import:
  - from /shared import tokens as shared-tokens
  - from ../billing import invoices
  - from /glossary import *
nav:
  order: 10
  visibility: public
---
```

Diagnostics concerning a frontmatter property are reported at the source
position of the offending key or value, whenever that position can be
determined. If a more specific source position cannot be determined, the
diagnostic is reported at the beginning of the frontmatter block. Exactly:

1. A block that is not valid YAML, or not a mapping ([HMD009](#diagnostics)),
   is reported at line 1, column 1, the beginning of the block.
2. A malformed value of a top-level key is reported at that key.
3. A problem inside `nav` is reported at the offending key inside `nav`.
4. A malformed list entry, such as a tag, a `use` entry, or an `import`
   statement, is reported at the entry's first character: in a block list,
   the first character after `- `.
5. Anything else is reported at its nearest enclosing key.

### `tags` — what a card is about

A wikilink says where a card lives; a tag says what it is about. The two are
kept apart, because collapsing them breaks both.[^tag-slot]

- `tags` is a list of strings. A `/` separates hierarchy levels, so
  `area/backend` is a descendant of `area`. An empty tag is malformed. A
  malformed value is an error ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).
- Tags MUST NOT take part in resolution. A tag is not a link target, and a
  target beginning with `#` is malformed ([HMD010](#diagnostics)).
- What tags mean beyond their shape — tag pages, hierarchy queries — is not
  specified in this version.

[^tag-slot]: Only the slot is fixed, so authors can tag against a stable shape
    now.

### `use` — per-card feature toggles

- `use` accepts a string or a list of strings. Each entry names a feature;
  prefixing `no_` disables it. A feature not mentioned takes its project
  default.
- The only feature this version defines is **`autodiscovery`**: whether the
  recursive search runs at all.[^visible-deps]
- **`use` inherits.** A `use` list in a folder note applies to that directory's
  whole subtree; the nearest declaration wins, and a card overrides its
  directory.
- An unrecognized feature name MUST be reported as an error, not ignored
  ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).[^misspelling]

[^visible-deps]: With the recursive search off, every dependency a card has is
    visible in its header or in the link itself.

[^misspelling]: A dropped misspelling would hand the author the default while
    they believe they configured something else.

### `import` — explicit name binding

`import` is a list of one-line statements in the grammar below, not nested YAML.

```abnf
import-stmt = %s"from" WS ref WS %s"import" WS import-list
import-list = "*" / binding *( [ WS ] "," [ WS ] binding )
binding     = import-name [ WS %s"as" WS import-name ]
ref         = absolute / relative   ; never unqualified
import-name = 1*seg-char            ; with no whitespace in it
WS          = 1*( %x20 / %x09 )     ; spaces and tabs
```

- Whitespace at the start and the end of a statement is not part of it.
  Spaces and tabs MAY stand on either side of a comma: `a,b`, `a, b`, and
  `a , b` are the same list.
- An `import-name` MUST NOT contain whitespace, so a card whose name contains a
  space cannot be imported by name; a wildcard import reaches it.
- `from`, `import`, and `as` are case-sensitive.
- **A named binding** has three parts: the imported target, its local name,
  and an optional alias. In `from /shared import tokens as shared-tokens`, the
  imported target is the card `tokens` in the directory `/shared`, and the
  alias `shared-tokens` is its local name. Without an alias, the local name is
  the imported name itself. The local name resolves to that one card and to
  nothing else. It applies only to bare names, so a multi-segment target falls
  through it.
- **A wildcard** adds a search origin: the directory joins the paths an
  unqualified reference is probed against, after the card's own directory and
  its parents. It does not bind the directory's contents eagerly, so a card
  added there later is reachable without editing the importing card.
- A `ref` MUST be absolute or relative, never unqualified.[^unqualified-ref]
- An imported origin MUST be probed non-recursively.[^legible]
- Two named bindings of the same local name MUST be reported as an error
  ([HMD015](#diagnostics)).[^last-wins]
- An import that cannot resolve its target MUST be reported as an error at the
  declaration, not at first use ([HMD015](#diagnostics)). This includes an
  import whose directory exists but whose named card does not.[^header-defect]
- A malformed statement MUST be reported as an error ([HMD014](#diagnostics)).
- **`import` does not inherit.** An import in a folder note binds names for that
  file alone.[^no-inherit]

[^unqualified-ref]: An unqualified `ref` would need resolving by the very
    algorithm the import feeds.

[^legible]: Probing without recursion keeps the reachable set legible from a
    directory listing.

[^last-wins]: Two bindings of one name have no declaration order to appeal to,
    and last-wins is guessing.

[^header-defect]: A wrong import is a defect in the header whether or not the
    body uses it.

[^no-inherit]: Inherited imports would make a leaf card's reference resolvable
    only by reading a file its author may never have opened.

### `nav` — placement and publication

`nav` is a mapping with two keys, `order` and `visibility`, so that a further
placement key can be added later without a second spelling for the first. A
minimal valid `nav` sets one key:

```yaml
nav:
  order: 10
```

- **`order`** is an integer. Cards that set it sort ahead of cards that do not,
  ascending; ties fall back to the default order: folder notes first, then cards
  by root-relative path, ordered by Unicode code point as candidates are.
- **`visibility`** is `public` or `private`, and gates publication. Any other
  value MUST be reported as an error, not coerced ([HMD013](#diagnostics)).
- Publication is **opt-in**. A card reaches a published site only if its
  effective visibility is `public`; the default is `private`.[^opt-in]
- An unpublished card MUST NOT be registered at all: no page, no
  address.[^address]
- A Markdown card's effective visibility is `public`, and a publisher MUST NOT
  register it as a page of its own: it is published where its file is, by
  whatever publishes Markdown. A link or an embed to one points at the page the
  site builds from that file, and renders as an unresolved link where the site
  builds none.
- `visibility` **inherits** the way `use` does: the card's own value, then the
  nearest ancestor folder note that sets one, then the default. `public` on the
  root folder note therefore publishes the whole namespace, and a card opts out
  with its own `private`.
- A published card that links to an unpublished one MUST render the link as
  unresolved, and MUST be reported as a warning ([HMD017](#diagnostics)).
- A published card that **embeds** an unpublished one MUST NOT expand it. It
  degrades to the same unresolved link and the same warning. Expansion itself
  takes the publication predicate from its caller.[^private-bytes]
- A scalar `nav`, an unknown key inside it, or a non-integer `order` MUST be
  reported as an error ([HMD013](#diagnostics)). A scalar such as `nav: 10` is
  an attempt to set the order; accepted silently, it would set nothing, and
  the card would sort last.

In the example vault, `index.hmd` is `public`, so every card is published.

[^opt-in]: The two possible mistakes are not symmetric. A card that should
    have shipped and did not is visible to its author on the next build; one
    that shipped and should not have is a leak nobody looks for.

[^address]: A page reachable by typing its address is published, whatever the
    sidebar says.

[^private-bytes]: Copying a private card's bytes into a public page is the one
    outcome the gate exists to prevent. Rendering a card named directly is not
    the same situation as building a site, so expansion stays free of policy.

## Project configuration

The `.hmd/` directory marks the vault and holds its configuration. This version
reads exactly three settings from `.hmd/config.toml` — `location_path`,
`autodiscovery`, and `mode` — and MUST ignore the rest of the
file,[^ignore-unknown] with one exception: the key `wiki` is banned. An
implementation that reads a vault MUST reject a `.hmd/config.toml` that sets
`wiki`, and MUST NOT read the vault, with an error that names the key and its
replacement, `location_path`.[^location-path-was-wiki]

```toml
location_path = "doc/wiki"  # the namespace root, relative to the vault; default "."

[discovery]
autodiscovery = true        # whether the recursive search runs at all
mode = "both"               # both (default) | recursive — where it searches
```

- **`location_path`** is optional. By default, the namespace root is the vault
  directory itself. The setting specifies the namespace root as a path
  relative to the vault directory, with `/` as the separator. Its default
  value is `"."`. It moves where the cards start and nothing else: `.hmd/`
  stays in the vault. An explicit namespace root given through the command
  line, an API, or application configuration still overrides it.
- **`autodiscovery`** is the project default for the `use` feature of the same
  name. A card overrides it in either direction.
- **`mode`** decides where the recursive search looks: `both` searches the
  whole namespace; `recursive` searches only the directory of the source card
  and the directories below it.
- `mode` MUST NOT be settable per card. A card MAY enable or disable
  autodiscovery, but MUST NOT change the scope or ranking of the recursive
  search.
- The configuration file may be empty or absent. Without it, the namespace
  root is the vault, autodiscovery is on, and the mode is
  `both`.[^no-config]

[^location-path-was-wiki]: Before 0.1.0 this setting was called `wiki`, and
    defaulted to `doc/wiki`. The rename is a breaking change. The key is
    banned rather than ignored, so that an old configuration fails loudly
    instead of silently moving the namespace root to the vault. Rename the key
    to `location_path`.

Precedence, most specific first:[^card-first]

```text
1. the card's own `use`
2. `use` in the nearest ancestor folder note
3. [discovery] autodiscovery in .hmd/config.toml
4. the built-in default — autodiscovery is ON
```

[^ignore-unknown]: Ignoring unknown settings lets the full schema be specified
    later without invalidating existing trees.

[^no-config]: With no configuration at all, bare names find cards, which is
    what a new author expects.

[^card-first]: Frontmatter beats configuration because the card is the most
    local place an author can say what they mean.

## Embed expansion

An **embed** is any wikilink with a `!` in front. It is resolved by the
algorithm above and then expanded in place.

- An embed of a whole card MUST expand to the target's full body with its
  frontmatter block removed.
- An embed of a heading fragment MUST expand to the matched heading and every
  following line up to, but excluding, the next heading whose level is the
  same as or numerically lower than the matched heading's: a `##` section ends
  at the next `#` or `##` heading.
- An embed of a block fragment MUST expand to the anchored block with the
  trailing anchor stripped. When the anchor is on a heading's line, the block is
  that heading's section, exactly as for a heading fragment.[^anchor-section]
- Expansion MUST be recursive: embeds inside expanded content are expanded too.
- A link or embed inside expanded content MUST be resolved as if it were read
  in the card it was written in: from that card's directory, with that card's
  imports and its own `use` toggles.[^stable-links]
- An embed cycle occurs when an expansion attempts to embed a card or section
  that is already being expanded within the same expansion chain. It is an
  error ([HMD007](#diagnostics)). Cycle detection is based on the identity of
  the resolved target, rather than the spelling of the reference: different
  references that resolve to the same card or section do not bypass it.
  Repeated embeds in independent expansion chains do not constitute a cycle.
- The starting card has an embed depth of zero. Each nested embed increases
  the depth by one. The depth limit is **16**.[^depth] If expanding an embed
  would exceed the depth limit, an error ([HMD008](#diagnostics)) is reported
  at the source position of that embed.
- Expansion MUST NOT shift heading levels: they arrive exactly as the source
  wrote them.[^no-shift]

In the example vault, `![[setup#^setup-linux]]` and `![[setup#Linux]]` expand to
the same text: the `## Linux` heading and its paragraph, up to `## macOS`.

[^anchor-section]: An anchor on a heading is that heading's stable name, so it
    embeds what the heading embeds.

[^stable-links]: Embedding a card must never change what its links mean.

[^depth]: Sixteen levels are deep enough for real composition and shallow
    enough to bound work on adversarial input.

[^no-shift]: Leaving level shifting out keeps expansion textual and
    reversible.

## Diagnostics

Rule identifiers are stable and MUST NOT be renumbered once
released.[^cited-ids] This version has no way to silence a diagnostic.

Diagnostic identifiers, severity levels, reporting conditions, and source
positions are defined by this standard. Human-readable diagnostic messages are
not standardized and may differ between implementations.

| ID | Severity | Condition | Defined in |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| HMD001 | warning | A link target does not resolve | [Rules the algorithm must satisfy](#rules-the-algorithm-must-satisfy) |
| HMD002 | error | An unqualified reference matches more than one card in the recursive search | [Rules the algorithm must satisfy](#rules-the-algorithm-must-satisfy) |
| HMD003 | error | A target lies outside the namespace root or inside a nested vault | [The namespace](#the-namespace) |
| HMD004 | error | A heading fragment is not present in the resolved card | [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors) |
| HMD005 | error | A block identifier is not present in the resolved card | [Fragments](#fragments) |
| HMD006 | error | A block anchor identifier is used twice in one card | [Block anchors](#block-anchors) |
| HMD007 | error | An embed would expand a card or section already being expanded in the same chain | [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion) |
| HMD008 | error | An embed would exceed the depth limit of 16 | [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion) |
| HMD009 | error | A frontmatter block is not valid YAML, or not a top-level mapping | [Frontmatter block](#frontmatter-block) |
| HMD010 | error | A wikilink or embed is malformed | [Grammar](#grammar) |
| HMD011 | warning | A heading has the same non-empty normalized identifier as an earlier one in the card, and no block anchor | [Heading anchors](#heading-anchors) |
| HMD012 | warning | A card shadows a module's folder note of the same name | [Resolving a reference within a directory](#resolving-a-reference-within-a-directory) |
| HMD013 | error | A `use` feature is unrecognized, or a `tags` or `nav` value is malformed | [`tags` — what a card is about](#tags-what-a-card-is-about), [`use` — per-card feature toggles](#use-per-card-feature-toggles), [`nav` — placement and publication](#nav-placement-and-publication) |
| HMD014 | error | An `import` statement is malformed | [`import` — explicit name binding](#import-explicit-name-binding) |
| HMD015 | error | An import cannot resolve its target, or two named imports bind one local name | [`import` — explicit name binding](#import-explicit-name-binding) |
| HMD016 | warning | An unqualified reference matches in more than one imported search path, and the earliest wins | [Rules the algorithm must satisfy](#rules-the-algorithm-must-satisfy) |
| HMD017 | warning | A published card links to or embeds one that is not published | [`nav` — placement and publication](#nav-placement-and-publication) |
| HMD018 | warning | A card shadows a card of the same name with a later card extension | [Resolving a reference within a directory](#resolving-a-reference-within-a-directory) |
| HMD019 | error | A file with a card extension is not valid UTF-8 | [Encoding](#encoding) |

- Each diagnostic MUST carry the rule identifier, the severity, a root-relative
  path with POSIX separators, a line, a column, and a message. An ambiguity MUST
  also carry its sorted candidate list.
- Diagnostics MUST be emitted sorted by path, ordered by Unicode code point as
  candidates are, then by line, then column, then rule.
- Implementations may provide a strict mode that treats warnings as errors for
  purposes of validation, automated testing, or continuous integration. Strict
  mode does not change the interpretation of HyperMarkDown documents or the
  diagnostic severity levels defined by this standard.
- Nothing beyond these rules is checked. The language checks the graph, the
  frontmatter, and the encoding, and makes no claim about prose, style, or
  inherited Markdown.

The severities follow from the rules. An unresolved reference is a warning
because writing forward is permitted, and the report is a to-do list. Several
matches in the recursive search are an error because that search has no
ranking rule. Every kind of shadowing is a warning — an earlier imported search
path (HMD016), an earlier card extension (HMD018), a card beside a module's
folder note (HMD012) — because a fixed order decides it and the shadowed card
stays reachable.

[^cited-ids]: Continuous integration configuration and documentation cite rule
    identifiers, so they never move.

## Rendering

The language does not specify a renderer. Two constraints are nonetheless
normative, because a renderer that breaks them changes what the document means:

- A target that does not resolve MUST still render as a link, marked as
  unresolved, rather than being dropped or raising an error.[^work-item]
- Embed expansion MUST follow [Embed expansion](#embed-expansion), including the
  depth and cycle bounds, whatever the output format.

An implementation MAY erase the graph on output — resolving every link to a path
and expanding every embed — producing ordinary Markdown or HTML that a reader
can use without knowing the format exists. Erasure is lossy: the boundary
between a card and the content it embedded is gone, and so is the source of
each link. Erasure suits shipping, and not anything that must show a reader
where content came from.

[^work-item]: A missing card is a work item; erasing the author's reference
    hides it.

## Cells

A **cell** is a fenced code block that a tool runs or draws. Its kind is the
first word of the fence's info string, and the cell kinds are exactly:

| Kind | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `hql#eval` | an [HQL](https://hql-lang.org/) query whose output is saved in the card |
| `hql#query` | an HQL query that runs for the cells that read it, and keeps no output |
| `d2#show` | a diagram in D2 |
| `mermaid#show` | a diagram in Mermaid |

- Kinds MUST be matched exactly and case-sensitively. A fence of any other kind
  is an ordinary fenced block.
- A plain `hql`, `d2`, or `mermaid` fence is a **listing**: source shown as
  code, never run and never drawn. A listing is how a card shows such source
  without invoking it.
- A cell's body is excluded from recognition like that of every fenced block.
  It yields no link, no embed, no anchor, and no edge in the graph.
- An `hql#eval` cell MAY be followed by its saved output, between an
  `<!-- hql:output … -->` and an `<!-- /hql:output -->` marker. The output is
  ordinary card content: a link in it is a link like any other. A card with
  cells and saved outputs is a well-formed card.
- The syntax inside a cell, its attributes, the output markers, and the hash
  that tells a current output from a stale one are HQL's, and are specified
  there.

*Suggested presentation, not a rule.* Show a cell's output or drawing first,
with its source behind a toggle. Run an HQL cell only when the reader asks, and
write its output into the card. Draw a diagram cell when the card opens and
again when the reader asks, and never write the drawing into the card.

## Extended Markdown syntax

HyperMarkDown extends CommonMark 0.31.2 with the following language
constructs. Their syntax and semantics are based on the corresponding
specifications referenced below.

| Construct | Syntax | Reference |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tables | pipe-delimited rows, with a delimiter row under the header | GitHub Flavored Markdown |
| Task lists | `- [ ] open` and `- [x] done` | GitHub Flavored Markdown |
| Strikethrough | `~~text~~`, with a double tilde | GitHub Flavored Markdown |
| Subscript | `~text~`, with a single tilde | PyMdown Extensions |
| Footnotes | `text[^1]`, with `[^1]: the note` elsewhere | PHP Markdown Extra |
| Callouts | `!!! note "Title"`, then a body indented four spaces | Python-Markdown |
| Collapsible callouts | `??? note` closed, `???+ note` open | PyMdown Extensions |
| Math | `$…$` inline, `$$…$$` on lines of its own | TeX |
| Attribute lists | `{: .class }` after a block | Python-Markdown |
| Markdown inside HTML | `<div markdown>` | Python-Markdown |
| Drawn diagrams | a `d2#show` or `mermaid#show` cell | D2, Mermaid |

A single tilde is subscript, and only a double tilde is strikethrough. GitHub
Flavored Markdown also reads a single tilde as strikethrough; HyperMarkDown
does not.

## Not in this version

The following are **deferred**, specified nowhere yet and not to be inferred
from their absence: inline properties, highlights, templates and parameterized
transclusion, backlinks and generated category pages, and heading-level
shifting on embed. Querying the document graph is
[HQL](https://hql-lang.org/)'s, a language of its own; a card carries it in
cells.

The following is **reserved**: a cross-namespace address of the form
`namespace:path/to/card`. The spelling is claimed so that it cannot be given
another meaning, and an implementation of this version MUST treat it as a
malformed target.

The gap an author is most likely to meet is the absence of an **escape
mechanism**, in both directions. A target cannot contain a reserved character,
and the only way to show a construct without invoking it is to put it in a code
span or a fenced block. Raw inline HTML is not excluded from recognition and is
therefore not a workaround.
